    <h2>The Mechanic: Pressure, Not Pull</h2>
    <p>In this framework, gravity is not a fundamental force mediated by a particle, nor is it a geometric well. It is emergent pressure: the inward pressure of the Timeless Sea (TSC) acting on our universe from outside.</p>
    
    <p>When a mass accumulates, it deepens the timeline coupling $\Gamma(\rho)$. The TSC responds with a boundary stress gradient. The scalar pressure $P(\rho)$ satisfies the Poisson-type relation:</p>
    
    <div style="background: var(--bg-secondary); padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px; border-left: 4px solid var(--accent); margin: 1.5rem 0; font-family: monospace; font-size: 1.1rem; text-align: center;">
      &nabla;&sup2;P = 4&pi;G&rho;
    </div>

    <p>Objects near a mass are not "falling" into a well. They are being <strong>pushed toward it</strong> by the Timeless Sea pressure differential. The "bulge" is the forward-facing temporal drag created when mass obstructs the natural flow of the substrate.</p>

    <h2>Why the Math is Identical</h2>
    <p>In the weak-field limit, both the standard geometric model and the TSC pressure model yield the exact same observable acceleration ($g = -\nabla\Phi$). However, the causal architecture is fundamentally different. By decoupling gravitational acceleration from time dilation and attributing both to matter density $\rho$, the framework generates novel, falsifiable predictions—such as qubit coherence scaling with gravitational isolation and atomic clock drifts near dark matter overdensities.</p>
